{-
   A string literal outliving the frame that wrote it - `lib/Native/Pointer.yana`'s declaration of
   `addressOf`, again.

   `PointerAddress.yana`'s question read from the other end. `"abc"` is placed bytes plus a call of
   the library's `stringLiteral` over *their address*, and how that address is taken is the read the
   compiler makes of the bytes global. Written `addressOf(it: source'a)` the read would be a loan and
   the address the global's; under `lib/`'s `addressOf(it: a)` an unpublished position answers a
   trivially copyable argument with a **copy**, so the three bytes went into a frame slot and the
   `String` described that slot.

   Which is invisible wherever the literal is inlined into its caller - the slot is then the caller's
   frame and outlives the read by luck - so every case below keeps the literal behind a call the
   inliner is told to leave alone. `newArrayStrings`, the enum name tables, `Date`'s day names and
   `Terminal`'s box drawing were thirteen cases of the library's suite reading text of the right
   length and the wrong bytes.

   This compiles clean and answers the same string every time, which is the point: the compiler has
   never copied the bytes here.
-}

import Test

@noinline fn literalBehindACall() -> String = "abc"

@noinline fn literalOfIndex(i: Size) -> String = "xy"

@test fn literalSurvivesItsFrame():
    let s = literalBehindACall()
    checkEqual(s, "abc")
    checkEqual(truncate(length(s)) :: Int, 3)

    -- And again after another frame has been and gone over the same stack bytes.
    let t = literalBehindACall()
    let u = literalOfIndex(0)
    checkEqual(t, "abc")
    checkEqual(u, "xy")

-- The shape `Core.newArrayStrings` reaches: a literal produced through a function value, stored in a
-- container, and read back after every frame that made one has returned.
@test fn literalsThroughAFunctionValue():
    let &xs = newArray(4, literalOfIndex)

    checkEqual(truncate(length(xs)) :: Int, 4)
    checkEqual(xs[0], "xy")
    checkEqual(xs[3], "xy")
